ng as food, to fill the burrow, storing, / being like the striving rats, their own / wealth asserting, those without a generous mind, / may there be no close friendship with them! / When a fierce-eyed wild boar fell on its left side, / not eating it there on that day, on the next day, / as the caves of the great mountain echoed, rising to hunt, / felling a dark male elephant on its good right side, / like a hungry tiger with an unyielding mind, / with the friendship of those possessing strength of character, / may the days spent together exist!","translation":"May we never have friendship with those mindless people who, like busy rats that search out small patches of ripe grain and carry the curved ears to stuff their burrows, only boast of their hoarded wealth. But may our days be spent in the company of those strong-willed, noble men who are like the hungry tiger: when a fierce wild boar falls to its left (an inauspicious side), the tiger refuses to eat it and goes hungry that day, but on the next day, rising with a passion that echoes through the mountain caves, it fells a massive dark male elephant on its victorious right side.","modern":"விளைந்த தானியங்கள் உள்ள சிறிய இடத்தைத் தேடி, வளைந்த கதிர்களைத் தங்களுக்கு உணவாகக் கொண்டு, தங்கள் வளைகளில் நிறையச் சேர்த்து வைக்கும் எலிகளைப் போன்ற முயற்சியை உடையவராகி, தங்களிடம் உள்ள செல்வத்தின் பெருமையைப் பேசும் நெஞ்சமில்லாதவர்களோடு நட்பு நமக்கு ஏற்படாமல் இருக்கட்டும்! கொடிய கண்களை உடைய பன்றி இடப்பக்கமாக விழுந்து இறந்ததால், அன்று அதனை உண்ணாமல் பசியோடு இருந்து, மறுநாளில் பெரிய மலைக்குகைகள் அதிரும்படி வேட்டைக்கு எழுந்து, கரிய ஆண் யானையைத் தனது வலப்பக்கத்தில் வீழ்த்தும் பசியுள்ள புலியைப் போன்ற தளராத நெஞ்சுறுதி உடையவர்களின் நட்போடு நாம் கூடி வாழும் நாட்கள் நமக்கு வாய்க்கட்டும்!","explanation":"This poem belongs to the Potuviyal (general) category of Puṟanāṉūṟu, composed by the Chola king Nalluruttiraṉ. The poet contrasts two types of people using vivid animal imagery: the small-minded who hoard wealth like rats, and the noble-minded who possess the pride and determination of a tiger. Rats laboriously gather small grains just to fill their own holes and boast of their possessions; similarly, petty people hoard wealth and lack a generous heart. In contrast, the tiger is so proud that it rejects a prey (the wild boar) that falls on the left side (considered inauspicious or unworthy), choosing to starve instead, but later hunts a mighty elephant on its right side. The key words are 'எலிமுயன்றனையர்' (those like striving rats) and 'புலிபசித் தன்ன' (like a hungry tiger), which beautifully illustrate the difference between mere survivalist hoarding and dignified, strong-willed ambition.","model":"gemini-3.5-flash"},"timing":{"male":[[0.0,0.8],[0.8,0.9],[0.9,1.3],[1.3,1.74],[1.8,2.5],[2.5,2.6],[2.6,2.8],[2.8,3.3],[3.3,3.5],[3.5,4.9],[4.98,5.05],[5.12,5.42],[5.58,6.38],[6.38,6.66],[6.66,6.95],[6.95,6.95],[6.95,8.1],[8.1,9.2],[9.2,9.5],[9.5,9.8],[9.8,10.0],[10.0,11.0],[11.0,11.1],[11.1,11.5],[11.5,11.9],[11.9,13.1],[13.1,13.5],[13.5,13.6],[13.6,13.8],[13.8,14.2],[14.2,15.5],[15.5,16.0],[16.0,16.1],[16.1,17.0],[17.72,18.42],[18.42,18.62],[18.62,19.32],[19.32,19.72],[19.72,21.72],[21.72,21.82],[21.82,22.02],[22.02,22.42],[22.42,23.12],[23.12,23.42],[23.42,23.7],[24.27,25.12],[25.12,25.22],[25.22,25.62],[25.62,25.92]],"female":[[0.0,0.62],[0.7,1.2],[1.2,1.4],[1.4,1.93],[2.0,2.7],[2.7,2.8],[2.8,3.1],[3.1,3.5],[3.5,3.7],[3.7,4.98],[4.98,5.4],[5.6,5.9],[5.9,6.8],[6.8,7.1],[7.1,7.4],[7.4,7.4],[7.4,8.6],[8.6,9.7],[9.7,10.1],[10.1,10.2],[10.2,10.6],[10.6,11.8],[11.8,11.9],[11.9,12.5],[12.5,12.9],[12.9,14.1],[14.1,14.4],[14.4,14.6],[14.6,14.8],[14.8,15.2],[15.2,16.5],[16.5,17.1],[17.1,17.1],[17.1,18.0],[18.88,19.68],[19.68,19.78],[19.78,20.48],[20.48,20.88],[20.88,22.29],[22.29,22.29],[22.29,22.98],[22.98,23.48],[23.48,24.48],[24.48,24.88],[24.88,25.18],[25.18,25.38],[25.38,26.98],[26.98,27.18],[27.18,27.38]]},"sync":[[["விளைபதம்","ripe stage"]],[["சீறிடம்","small place"]],[["நோக்கி","having looked at"]],[["வளைக